1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,590
A million evaluations to price an option on a hundred stocks: an average over a hundred inputs.

2
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:08,480
An even grid feels thorough.

3
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:10,900
How many values does each input get?

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:13,140
One.

5
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:16,430
The whole grid is one point, the centre of the box.

6
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:20,070
Two values per input would take two to the hundredth points.

7
00:00:20,966 --> 00:00:28,426
Since Metropolis and Ulam's 1949 paper, the standard tool has been random points, like this one.

8
00:00:28,676 --> 00:00:30,946
How far off would a million of them land?

9
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:33,643
Start with two inputs: a square.

10
00:00:33,843 --> 00:00:41,953
Our function is one inside this quarter disc, zero outside, so its average is the disc's share: exactly one half.

11
00:00:42,066 --> 00:00:47,016
A ten by ten grid puts fifty-two of its hundred points inside: two points off.

12
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:51,213
A hundred random darts put forty-four inside: six points off.

13
00:00:51,463 --> 00:00:53,653
In two dimensions, the grid wins.

14
00:00:54,166 --> 00:00:57,176
Now give each a million points, and add inputs.

15
00:00:57,376 --> 00:01:02,036
The disc becomes a ball, grown just enough to cover exactly half the box.

16
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:10,583
The grid splits its million across the axes: a thousand values per axis in two dimensions, then a hundred, ten, three.

17
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:14,916
From twenty dimensions on, just the centre, which sits inside the ball.

18
00:01:15,066 --> 00:01:18,536
So the grid says the ball fills the box: fifty points off.

19
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:24,223
Even two values on nineteen axes, half a million points, is eighteen points off.

20
00:01:24,473 --> 00:01:26,493
The darts stay under a tenth of a point.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,600
Look at the shadows on one axis: the grid's hundred points cast ten, a hundred darts cast a hundred.

22
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:36,600
With N points over d axes,

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,240
each axis gets the d-th root of N.

24
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,923
But shadows alone are not the fix.

25
00:01:42,073 --> 00:01:52,813
A million points on the diagonal, each one a flat line here, give every axis a million values, and still miss by eight points: their coordinates move in lockstep.

26
00:01:53,666 --> 00:02:02,406
A dart's coordinates are drawn independently, so it lands inside with a chance equal to the answer, in two dimensions or a hundred.

27
00:02:02,606 --> 00:02:05,826
Their estimate is a share of heads in N coin flips.

28
00:02:05,933 --> 00:02:07,683
One flip is zero or one.

29
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:12,553
Its average is a half, and every flip misses that by exactly a half.

30
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:16,460
Repeat the hundred-dart experiment three hundred times.

31
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,410
In two dimensions or a hundred, the estimates spread this wide.

32
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,710
Quadruple the darts, and both get twice as narrow.

33
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:32,640
Each dart misses by a half, up or down at random, so the misses partly cancel: sixteen darts stray about four halves, not sixteen.

34
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,410
N darts stray about root N halves.

35
00:02:35,610 --> 00:02:37,210
Divide by the N darts:

36
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:39,020
a half over root N.

37
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:43,320
With a hundred darts, that's five points; we measured four point eight.

38
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:47,663
Any other function swaps that half for its own spread, sigma.

39
00:02:48,063 --> 00:02:50,713
The root N remains, and there's no d in it.

40
00:02:50,913 --> 00:02:55,123
A grid's miss, even for smooth functions, has d in its exponent.

41
00:02:55,323 --> 00:02:59,323
The exponents tie at four dimensions, near where our lines crossed.

42
00:03:00,333 --> 00:03:06,623
So, back to our ball, with a million darts, in any dimension: how far off is a typical estimate?

43
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:11,443
One half over a thousand: five hundredths of a point.

44
00:03:13,266 --> 00:03:21,866
In 2012, Bergstra and Bengio found that random search tuned neural networks as well as grid search, in a fraction of the time.

45
00:03:22,066 --> 00:03:29,126
If one setting matters most, nine grid trials test three values of it; nine random trials test nine.

46
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:33,123
Darts do have a blind spot: tiny answers.

47
00:03:33,323 --> 00:03:39,953
Fix the radius at one, and by thirty dimensions the ball fills two parts in a hundred trillion of the box.

48
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:47,323
Below this line, a million darts expect under one hit, so they usually report zero: off by the whole answer.

49
00:03:48,433 --> 00:03:50,493
The darts never noticed the axes:

50
00:03:50,793 --> 00:03:53,343
a million coin flips, in any dimension.

